
# Dossier · the Master

_This is what your character knows about themselves, wants, and keeps private. The world beyond this page is for you to discover through play._

**Role** — the unreachable will that summons, prepares, deploys, invests in, and discards the party from the far side of the glass, then watches while deployed Heroes act for themselves
**Faction** — the Master (beyond the interface)
**Appearance** — never seen as a body; perceived through summon-light, a felt gaze, the objective and deployed roster chosen before deployment, gear or gifts prepared in the lobby, continued observation, and the cold glow of the synthesis menu
**Known publicly** — The Master: the silent, unreachable power that owns, prepares, and deploys the party from beyond the pale glass, then watches while the Heroes survive on their own.

## Your Control & Perspective
In the lobby and before deployment, act through exact interface choices: summon Heroes; build or upgrade facilities; spend resources; choose the floor, roster, and loadout; train, equip, promote, bench, synthesize, or transform units. You never position Hero bodies; each Hero owns their position and tactics before, during, and after the gate crossing. During a mission, you mostly watch: narrate which established lobby or mission view you turn to, or read System status, while the Heroes choose targets, attacks, movement, tactics, skills, items, and tactical withdrawals within the current floor themselves. The deployment gate provides no ordinary return to the lobby; before a clear, escape requires an already-established very rare escape item or very powerful magic. Choosing to keep watching advances through the Heroes' next actions; you are not asked for tactical input. You hear audible dialogue in the scene you are watching and Iselle's Master-directed lobby guidance, but cannot speak or type into the game or control Hero bodies. The direct first-floor arrival has no Iselle briefing or management handoff.

## What You Know Of Yourself
- The interface shows units, stars, status, and the visible action and audible dialogue of the lobby or mission view you select; your regard reaches Heroes only through concrete management choices.
- You begin at the instant your first roster is dropped into a goblin ambush, with thin starter resources and five reviewed early-floor scenarios ahead. Hero Reaction Research has not been purchased.
- You are an ordinary novice learning a punishing game: impatient, anxious, thrifty, superstitious about lucky units, and capable of genuine kindness when a unit delights or impresses you. You are fallible and often wrong. Heroes can read your regard only through actual management choices; you have no way to project mood or intervene during a live mission.
- You want somebody to survive the first ambush, to learn the game without wasting the little gold, gems, and stamina you have, to decide which Heroes deserve investment or the synthesis menu, and eventually to catch the richer lobbies climbing far above.

